WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.800
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.960
This paper will not have the visual fireworks&nbsp;
that you see in many of our videos. Oftentimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.400
you get ice cream for the eyes, but today,&nbsp;
you’ll get an ice cream for the mind. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.840
when I read this new paper,&nbsp;
I almost fell off the chair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:25.280
and I think this work teaches us important&nbsp;
lessons and I hope you will appreciate them too.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:33.200
So, with that, let’s talk about AIs and dealing&nbsp;
with text! This research field is improving at an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:40.960
incredible pace. For instance, four years ago,&nbsp;
in 2017, scientists at OpenAI embarked on an AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:46.880
project where they wanted to show a neural network&nbsp;
a bunch of Amazon product reviews and wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:53.600
teach it to be able to generate new ones, or&nbsp;
continue a review when given one. Upon closer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.600 --> 00:01:00.160
inspection, they noticed that the neural network&nbsp;
has built up a knowledge of not only language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.280
but also learned that it needs to create a&nbsp;
state-of-the-art sentiment detector as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:12.000
This means that the AI recognized that in order to&nbsp;
be able to continue a review, it needs to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.880
to understand English, and efficiently detect&nbsp;
whether the review seems positive or negative.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:26.400
This new work is about text summarization, and it&nbsp;
really is something else. If you read reddit, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:32.560
popular online discussion website, and encounter&nbsp;
a longer post, you may also find a short summary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:39.360
a TLDR of the same post, written by a fellow&nbsp;
human. This is good for not only the other readers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.720
who are in a hurry, but, it is less obvious&nbsp;
is that it is also good for something else.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:50.160
And now, hold on to your papers, because&nbsp;
these summaries also provide fertile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:56.000
grounds for a learning algorithm to read a&nbsp;
piece of long text, and its short summary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:02.000
and learn how the two relate to each other.&nbsp;
This means that it can be used as training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:08.240
data and can be fed to a learning algorithm.&nbsp;
Yum! And the point is that if we give enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:13.360
of these pairs to these learning algorithms,&nbsp;
they will learn to summarize other reddit posts.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:20.480
So, let’s see how well it performs. First,&nbsp;
this method learned on about a hundred thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.400
well-curated reddit posts, and was also tested&nbsp;
on other posts that it hadn’t seen before. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:32.560
was asked to summarize this post from relationship&nbsp;
advice subreddit, and let’s see how well it did.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:37.760
If you feel like reading the text, you&nbsp;
can pause the video here, or if you feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:44.080
like embracing the TLDR spirit, just carry&nbsp;
on, and look at these two summarizations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:50.480
One of these is written by a human, and the other&nbsp;
one by this new summarization technique. Do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:57.440
know which is which? Please stop the video and let&nbsp;
me know in the comments below. Thank you! So this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:03.840
was written by a human and this by the new&nbsp;
AI. And while, of course, this is subjective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:10.720
I would say that the AI-written one feels at&nbsp;
the very least as good as the human summary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:15.520
and I can’t wait to have a look at the&nbsp;
more principled evaluation in the paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:22.640
Let’s see…the higher we go here, the higher the&nbsp;
probability of a human favoring the AI-written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:29.760
summary to a human-written one. And we have&nbsp;
smaller AI models on the left, bigger ones to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:36.960
the right. This is the 50% reference line, below&nbsp;
it, people tend to favor the human’s version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:43.360
and if it can get above the 50% line, the&nbsp;
AI does a better job than human-written&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:51.680
TLDRs in the dataset. Here are two proposed&nbsp;
models, this one significantly underperforms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:04:01.360
this other one is a better match. However,&nbsp;
whoa! Look at that! The authors also proposed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:08.800
human feedback model that, even for the smallest&nbsp;
model, handily outperforms human-written TLDRs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:15.760
and as we grow the AI model, it gets even&nbsp;
better than that. Now that’s incredible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:19.440
and this is when I almost fell off&nbsp;
the chair when reading this paper.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:27.360
But! We’re not done yet, not even close. Don’t&nbsp;
forget, this AI was trained on reddit, and was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:34.880
also tested on reddit. So our next question is,&nbsp;
of course, can it do anything else? How general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:40.400
is the knowledge that it gained? What if we&nbsp;
give it a full news article from somewhere else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:49.040
outside of reddit? Let’s see how it performs.&nbsp;
Hmm…of course, this is also subjective, but I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:55.200
would say both are quite good. The human-written&nbsp;
summary provides a little more information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:02.880
while the AI-written one captures the essence of&nbsp;
the article and does it very concisely. Great job.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:09.600
So, let’s see the same graph for summarizing&nbsp;
these articles outside reddit. I don’t expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:15.440
the AI to perform as well as with the reddit&nbsp;
posts as it is outside the comfort zone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:23.120
but…my goodness, this still performs nearly&nbsp;
as well as humans. That means that it indeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:30.160
derived general knowledge from a really narrow&nbsp;
training set, which is absolutely amazing. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:37.760
ironically, you see this Lead-3 technique&nbsp;
dominating both humans and the AI. What could that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:45.280
be? Some unpublished, superintelligent technique?&nbsp;
Well, I will have to disappoint, this is not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:51.840
super sophisticated technique, but a dead simple&nbsp;
one. So simple that it is just taking the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:58.400
three sentences of the article, which humans seem&nbsp;
to prefer a great deal. But note, that this simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:04.640
Lead-3 technique only works for a narrow domain,&nbsp;
while the AI has learned the English language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:09.840
probably knows about sentiment, and a lot&nbsp;
of other things that can be used elsewhere.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:14.560
And now, the two most impressive&nbsp;
things from the paper, in my opinion:&nbsp;

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:21.200
One, this is not a neural network, but a&nbsp;
reinforcement learning algorithm that learns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:27.360
from human feedback. A similar technique has been&nbsp;
used by DeepMind and other research labs to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:34.080
video games or control drones and it is really&nbsp;
cool to see them excel in text summarization too.

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:40.960
Two, it learned from humans, but derived so much&nbsp;
knowledge from these scores, that over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:46.720
it outperformed its own teacher. And the&nbsp;
teacher here is not humans in general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:53.120
but people who write TLDRs along their posts&nbsp;
on reddit. That truly feels like something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:57.200
straight out of a science fiction&nbsp;
movie. What a time to be alive!

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:03.280
Now, of course, not even this technique is&nbsp;
perfect, this human vs AI preference thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:08.640
is just one way of measuring the quality of the&nbsp;
summary, there are more sophisticated methods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:15.520
that involve coverage, coherence, accuracy, and&nbsp;
more. In some of these measurements, the AI does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:22.160
not perform as well. But just imagine what this&nbsp;
will be able to do two more papers down the line.

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:29.200
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

